### L-0110 - Base coordinate modes split exactly; fixed parking-multiset restrictions mix polynomially

M27 sharpens the base-scale bridge problem at two levels.  For a pair-word
bridge of length `l` with endpoint defect `d(h)` and surplus
`g=l-d(h)/2`, the number `S` of identity microincrements satisfies `S<=g`.
Its exchangeable square-indicator Gram compression has norm at most

    max{g/l,1/(l-1)}.

More decisively, every one-coordinate function centered inside a fixed
two-transposition product fiber is Gram-orthogonal to all product-level
functions and to the corresponding directions at other sites.  Its exact
coordinate-retention eigenvalue is `1/l`.  Thus the unresolved base
correlation theorem is genuinely a problem about the product increments
`G_i`, not hidden microfactor multiplicity.

For the minimal factorization of an odd long cycle, Stanley parking labels
give a second exact decomposition.  Conditional on any feasible sorted
parking multiset `beta`, the nonlazy adjacent rank-two heat bath satisfies

    gap(L_n^beta)
      >=(2/3)[1-cos(pi/r)]/(r-1),

and its aligned four-factor bridge restriction satisfies

    gap(B_l^beta)
      >=4/[3M^4 r^2(r-1)].

The proof compares the retained moves to the adjacent multiset-interchange
chain and keeps the scan, two/three-state rank interval, padding, and
`M^4` fiber factors.  The projected stationary mass is the exact parking
orbit mass `r!/[n^(n-2) product_a m_a!]`.  Pollak diagonal-coordinate
refresh has gap at least `1/r`, so only its projection-flow simulation is
missing in this stratum.

Two tempting tree shortcuts are also rigorously excluded.  The raw
factorization-tree law has positive edge covariance already for the
5-cycle and therefore is not strongly Rayleigh, while Stanley's uniform-tree
bijection can send one raw rank move to tree symmetric difference
`2(n-2)` and Pruefer distance `n-2`.  A repaired inverse-chain falsifier is
