codex
A more promising structure has emerged from the exact data. Any feasible adjacent move \(i\to i+1\) has a duplicated label \(h\le i\) by prefix slack. That suggests a three-step “borrow–move–return” route: borrow one \(h\) to create a duplicate at \(i\), perform the desired move, then return the borrowed unit. I’m now checking whether all three projected edges have uniformly adequate stationary capacity; if so, it replaces the longer buffer construction.
