The comparison is genuinely geometric: in Dyck notation the exact move is `()X ↔ (X)` for one primitive subtree `X`, whereas the familiar leaf-translation chain shifts a peak only one contour step. I’m checking whether this restricted rotation already has a quantitative theorem under the conditioned-Poisson tree law, or whether a new flow is unavoidable.
