M81 independently accepts the frozen theorem with zero material objection.
It verifies identity from the eight token trajectories, connectedness from
two generating voltage walks, and the all-`L` depth-two classification.  In
the base `K_(4,4)` rotation system exactly eight of 36 four-cycles have zero
voltage; every nonzero voltage has a coordinate `+/-1` and so stays nonzero
modulo every `L>=2`.  Each zero-voltage face has exactly four collision
orders, all inducing the same five interval endpoint maps, and deleting the
equal pair isolates the lifted face corner.  Thus all `32L^2` depth-two
collision orders are unsafe.  Exact checks through `L=8` agree.

At an arbitrary preliminary elimination state `(O,R)`, the next two outputs
coincide exactly when the two selected residual factors already form a
duplicate pair `R_i=R_j=p`.  Both orders leave the same deletion word

```text
O + R[:i] + p R[i+1:j] p + R[j+1:].
```

If `a` unordered duplicate pairs give a connected deletion word and
`s=|R|`, the conditional next-pair hit probability is exactly
`2a/[s(s-1)]`.  This reduces the unresolved row bound to
SAFE-DUPLICATE REACHABILITY / OCCUPATION.  Fixed-seed Monte Carlo found zero
safe hits in 50,000 `W_2` and 10,000 `W_3` orders, but that is explicitly not
a proof.  Exact witnesses also refute both “nonzero selected-prefix homology
is safe” and “every unsafe collision isolates one vertex.”  Even zero total
row probability for this particular braid law would refute only that
coarea law, not the heat-bath gap.

